    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p30-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Consider $f(x)=x^2+2x-3$ over the domain $[a,\\infty)$. Find the smallest value of $a$ for which $f$ has an inverse.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x^2+2x-3\\\\f(x)&=(x+1)^2-4\\end{align*}$$\nThe vertex has $x$-coordinate $-1$. The function is strictly increasing on $[-1,\\infty)$, so it is injective there. If $a<-1$, the domain includes points on both sides of the vertex with equal outputs. Hence the smallest value is $a=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Consider $g(x)=x^2-6x+1$ over the domain $(-\\infty,a]$. Find the largest value of $a$ for which $g$ has an inverse.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=x^2-6x+1\\\\g(x)&=(x-3)^2-8\\end{align*}$$\nThe vertex has $x$-coordinate $3$. The function is strictly decreasing on $(-\\infty,3]$, so it is injective there. If $a>3$, the domain includes points on both sides of the vertex with equal outputs. Hence the largest value is $a=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Consider $h(x)=\\sin(x)$ over the domain $[-a,a]$. Find the largest value of $a$ for which $h$ has an inverse.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.",
          "worked": "The sine function is strictly increasing on $[-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}]$, so it is injective there. Any larger symmetric interval includes points on both sides of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ with equal sine values: for sufficiently small $t>0$,\n$$\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-t\\right)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}+t\\right).$$\nHence the largest value is $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
